<article xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="review-article" dtd-version="1.3"><?properties open_access?><processing-meta base-tagset="archiving" mathml-version="3.0" table-model="xhtml" tagset-family="jats"><restricted-by>pmc</restricted-by></processing-meta><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">BJA Open</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">BJA Open</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>BJA Open</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="epub">2772-6096</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Elsevier</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta>
<article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">40822259</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">12351347</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pii">S2772-6096(25)00097-8</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.bjao.2025.100473</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">100473</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Review Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Innovative technologies in regional anaesthesia education: a scoping review</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au1"><name><surname>Chequer</surname><given-names>Samuel</given-names></name><email>samuel.chequer@nhs.net</email><xref rid="aff1" ref-type="aff">a</xref><xref rid="aff2" ref-type="aff">b</xref><xref rid="fn1" ref-type="fn">&#x02020;</xref></contrib><aff id="aff1"><label>a</label>Kings College Hospital, London, UK</aff><aff id="aff2"><label>b</label>Faculty of Medical Sciences, University College London (UCL), UK</aff></contrib-group><author-notes><fn id="fn1"><label>&#x02020;</label><p id="ntpara0010">Affiliated to University College London, UK, during the conduct of this review.</p></fn></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="pmc-release"><day>06</day><month>8</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><!-- PMC Release delay is 0 months and 0 days and was based on <pub-date
						pub-type="epub">.--><pub-date pub-type="collection"><month>9</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>06</day><month>8</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><volume>15</volume><elocation-id>100473</elocation-id><history><date date-type="received"><day>24</day><month>3</month><year>2025</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>17</day><month>6</month><year>2025</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; 2025 The Author(s)</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2025</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/).</license-p></license></permissions><abstract id="abs0010"><sec><title>Background</title><p>Regional anaesthesia has experienced a global resurgence over recent decades alongside increasing demand for anaesthetists to be competent in its delivery. This was reflected in a recent UK anaesthetic curriculum update, which mandates a wide range of specific regional anaesthesia competencies. Despite this, regional anaesthesia education remains <italic>ad hoc</italic> and inconsistent. Innovative technologies (artificial intelligence, virtual reality, and augmented reality) are increasingly integrated within medical education, and have the potential to transform training practices. This scoping review aimed to explore the ways in which innovative technologies are currently used in regional anaesthesia, and to consider their role in education and training.</p></sec><sec><title>Methods</title><p>This review was conducted using established frameworks for scoping reviews, and included searches of three major databases, alongside targeted citation searching. Data were analysed numerically, followed by reflexive thematic analysis.</p></sec><sec><title>Results</title><p>In total, 855 citations were identified. After removal of duplicates and abstract eligibility screening, 106 full-text articles were assessed and 38 met the criteria for inclusion. The majority of studies were published in the last 2 yr and a lack of high-quality evidence, particularly focussing on educational outcomes, was noted. A wide range of applications for innovative technologies in regional anaesthesia education were described including roles in anatomy education, accelerated skill acquisition, simulation-based medical education, and assessment.</p></sec><sec><title>Conclusions</title><p>Innovative technologies were associated with benefits such as provision of reliable learning experiences, reduced supervisory requirements, and enhanced educational outcomes. Future educators should consider their utility and provide structured evaluation. Significant heterogeneity was noted in the literature base and further research is recommended, specifically studying primary educational outcomes.</p></sec></abstract><kwd-group id="kwrds0010"><title>Keywords</title><kwd>artificial intelligence</kwd><kwd>assessment</kwd><kwd>augmented reality</kwd><kwd>innovative technology</kwd><kwd>regional anaesthesia</kwd><kwd>simulation</kwd><kwd>supervision</kwd><kwd>virtual reality</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta><notes><p id="misc0010">Handling editor: Dr Susan M. Goobie</p></notes></front><body><sec id="sec1"><title>Background</title><sec id="sec1.1"><title>Regional anaesthesia education</title><p id="p0010">Regional anaesthesia describes the injection of medications (usually local anaesthetic agents) near nerves to block neural transmissions providing patients with analgesia and/or surgical anesthesia. The practice of regional anesthesia was first described in the early 19th century and has recently experienced a global resurgence with the recognized advantage of resource efficiencies, obviating the need for general anaesthesia, and with the advent of safety advancements such as ultrasound (US)-guided techniques.<xref rid="bib1" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>, <xref rid="bib2" ref-type="bibr">2</xref>, <xref rid="bib3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref> Proficiency in regional anaesthesia is now considered a fundamental skill for the contemporary anaesthetist<xref rid="bib4" ref-type="bibr"><sup>4</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib5" ref-type="bibr"><sup>5</sup></xref> as reflected in the 2021 Royal College of Anaesthetists curriculum update.<xref rid="bib6" ref-type="bibr"><sup>6</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib7" ref-type="bibr"><sup>7</sup></xref></p><p id="p0015">Effective regional anaesthesia education and training is crucial to develop and maintain workforce proficiency.<xref rid="bib2" ref-type="bibr"><sup>2</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib4" ref-type="bibr"><sup>4</sup></xref> Appropriate regional anaesthesia knowledge and procedural skills are important in maximising the safety and success rates of these techniques.<xref rid="bib5" ref-type="bibr"><sup>5</sup></xref> Varied approaches to regional anaesthesia education are described in the literature, but conventional methods typically involve preclinical theoretical study, accompanied by guided practice on artificial or cadaveric models. These modes of learning are usually conducted in small groups, traditionally accessed through educational courses. This is followed by transition to learning in the clinical setting, where learners practise with direct expert supervision.<xref rid="bib2" ref-type="bibr"><sup>2</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib8" ref-type="bibr"><sup>8</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib9" ref-type="bibr"><sup>9</sup></xref> Learning via these methods can be limited by barriers such as supervisor expertise and availability, patient caseload, clinical risk, and access to educational courses.<xref rid="bib10" ref-type="bibr"><sup>10</sup></xref> The requirement for these factors to align to provide appropriate training opportunities can result in varied, inconsistent, and <italic>ad hoc</italic> exposure to educational experiences.<xref rid="bib11" ref-type="bibr"><sup>11</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib12" ref-type="bibr"><sup>12</sup></xref> This highlights a necessity for alternative methods of regional anaesthesia education which provide standardized, reliable, and consistent educational opportunities with minimised clinical risk.<xref rid="bib13" ref-type="bibr"><sup>13</sup></xref></p></sec><sec id="sec1.2"><title>Innovative technologies in medical education</title><p id="p0020">The integration of innovative technologies (as described in <xref rid="tbl1" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref>) in medical education is an expanding and well described field. Recent systematic reviews report a wide range of applications for innovative technologies with cited benefits such as enhanced educational outcomes, learner engagement, and reduced educational disparities. These findings were reported alongside calls for educators to prepare for an inevitable and exponential increase in their adoption.<xref rid="bib28" ref-type="bibr"><sup>28</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib29" ref-type="bibr"><sup>29</sup></xref> Within anaesthesia, most studies have focussed on the clinical utility for innovative technologies and their outcomes as educational tools have not yet been comprehensively investigated.<xref rid="bib8" ref-type="bibr"><sup>8</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib10" ref-type="bibr"><sup>10</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib30" ref-type="bibr">30</xref>, <xref rid="bib31" ref-type="bibr">31</xref>, <xref rid="bib32" ref-type="bibr">32</xref>, <xref rid="bib33" ref-type="bibr">33</xref><table-wrap position="float" id="tbl1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Working definitions for the scoping review of innovative technologies in regional anaesthesia education. AI, artificial intelligence; AR, augmented reality; VR, virtual reality.</p></caption><alt-text id="alttext0025">Table 1</alt-text><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Innovative technologies</td><td>AR and VR are commonly classified as &#x02018;immersive&#x02019; technologies, defined as those which &#x02018;blur[s] the boundary between the physical and virtual worlds and enable[s] users to experience a sense of immersion&#x02019;.<xref rid="bib14" ref-type="bibr"><sup>14</sup></xref> However, AI is often referred to alongside such &#x02018;immersive&#x02019; technologies, sometimes under the umbrella term &#x02018;innovative&#x02019; technologies.<xref rid="bib15" ref-type="bibr"><sup>15</sup></xref> For ongoing clarity of discussion, this review will adopt this description of innovative technologies, namely to include AI, VR, and AR</td></tr><tr><td>Artificial intelligence</td><td>Definitions of AI have evolved alongside technological development.<xref rid="bib16" ref-type="bibr"><sup>16</sup></xref> This review will adopt the contemporary definition proposed by Popenici and Kerr<xref rid="bib17" ref-type="bibr"><sup>17</sup></xref> which states that AI is &#x02018;computing systems that are able to engage in human-like processes such as learning, adapting, synthesising, self-correction and use of data for complex processing tasks&#x02019;.</td></tr><tr><td>Virtual reality</td><td>VR refers to technologies which surround the user in computer-generated virtual environments, whilst simulating the user&#x02019;s existence in a way that approximates reality.<xref rid="bib18" ref-type="bibr"><sup>18</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib19" ref-type="bibr"><sup>19</sup></xref> This most commonly involves the use of hardware such as headsets and microphones to engage visual and auditory senses, though can include haptic feedback to provide tactile sensations.<xref rid="bib20" ref-type="bibr"><sup>20</sup></xref></td></tr><tr><td>Augmented reality</td><td>AR allows virtual elements and information to be overlaid on the real world, appearing to the user as coexisting in reality.<xref rid="bib21" ref-type="bibr"><sup>21</sup></xref> This can be experienced through VR user interfaces such as headset devices, or through other mediums such as smartphones or tablets, and allows users to explore extensive real-world information in a streamlined way.<xref rid="bib22" ref-type="bibr"><sup>22</sup></xref> Within medical education, AR is usually used to augment learning in virtual environments or guide the user through complex procedures.<xref rid="bib23" ref-type="bibr"><sup>23</sup></xref></td></tr><tr><td>Regional anaesthesia</td><td>Regional anaesthesia involves the injection of medications (such as local anaesthetics) around or near to nerves to block neural transmissions.<xref rid="bib24" ref-type="bibr"><sup>24</sup></xref> It can be used to provide analgesia or surgical anaesthesia. Intravenous regional anaesthesia is a technique which been described, but is not a common practice and will not be considered in this review.</td></tr><tr><td>Anaesthetists</td><td>Anaesthetists (or anaesthesiologists) are specialist medical doctors who have completed or are undertaking postgraduate training in the subspeciality of anaesthesia. In the United Kingdom (UK), these are individuals who have completed a primary medical degree (e.g. Bachelor of Medicine and Bachelor of Surgery) and 2 years of foundation training, before enrolling in a postgraduate anaesthesia training programme. In the UK, the term also refers to speciality doctors, associate specialists, and clinical fellows in anaesthesia, who have completed at least 2 years of practice in anaesthesia, and may work independently or with consultant supervision.<xref rid="bib25" ref-type="bibr"><sup>25</sup></xref> In this scoping review, studies were included from outside the UK to maximise breadth of understanding. There exists a variety of heterogeneous anaesthesia training pathways globally, and thus terminology can be difficult to define.<xref rid="bib26" ref-type="bibr"><sup>26</sup></xref> For feasibility, this review defines anaesthetists as medical doctors who have completed or are completing training pathways recognised by the World Federation of Societies of Anaesthesiologists (WFSA), or those practicing anaesthesia with accreditation to a body recognised by the WFSA.<xref rid="bib27" ref-type="bibr"><sup>27</sup></xref></td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></p><p id="p0025">There is a paucity of evidence outlining the optimal delivery of regional anaesthesia education<xref rid="bib34" ref-type="bibr"><sup>34</sup></xref> and although the use of innovative technologies is promising, their specific impact on educational practices has not been systematically explored.<xref rid="bib35" ref-type="bibr"><sup>35</sup></xref> Research priorities within regional anaesthesia education have been identified as; simulation, curriculum, knowledge translation, methodology, and assessment.<xref rid="bib36" ref-type="bibr"><sup>36</sup></xref> The potential impact for innovative technologies in these areas is high, as demonstrated by their adoption in broader areas of education. To explore and map the evidence base, a scoping review was conducted to investigate the role for innovative technologies in augmenting regional anaesthesia educational practices.</p><p id="p0030">The conduct of this review was underpinned by an educationalist perspective, and key theories from the medical education literature were used to explore the findings and relate them back to changing practice in regional anaesthesia education.</p></sec></sec><sec id="sec2"><title>Methods</title><sec id="sec2.1"><title>Protocol</title><p id="p0035">The study protocol for this manuscript was informed by the scoping review framework set out by Arksey and O&#x02019;Malley<xref rid="bib37" ref-type="bibr"><sup>37</sup></xref> with methodological adaptions from Levac and colleagues<xref rid="bib38" ref-type="bibr"><sup>38</sup></xref> and Tricco and colleagues.<xref rid="bib39" ref-type="bibr"><sup>39</sup></xref> It was developed in line with guidance from the Joanna Briggs Institute<xref rid="bib40" ref-type="bibr"><sup>40</sup></xref> and reporting was aligned with the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses extension for scoping reviews (PRISMA-ScR).<xref rid="bib39" ref-type="bibr"><sup>39</sup></xref></p></sec><sec id="sec2.2"><title>Prior scoping reviews</title><p id="p0040">At commencement of this study on 26 April 2024, registries and databases (Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews, Medline, Scopus, Web of Science) were checked for existing scoping reviews and none were returned that addressed the research aims.</p></sec><sec id="sec2.3"><title>Research question</title><p id="p0045">Research aims were informed by the authors lived experiences and targeted literature searches were used to substantiate assumptions and define the scope of inquiry. Aims were developed into formal research questions using problematisation.<xref rid="bib41" ref-type="bibr"><sup>41</sup></xref> The FINERMAPS (feasible, interesting, novel, ethical, relevant, manageable, appropriate, potential value, publishability, and systematic) framework<xref rid="bib42" ref-type="bibr"><sup>42</sup></xref> was applied to ensure fulfilment of specific criteria such as feasibility, relevance, and appropriateness. Questions were then refined using the population/concept/context (PCC) model with components defined as &#x02018;population&#x02019; (anaesthetists), &#x02018;concept&#x02019; (innovative technologies), and &#x02018;context&#x02019; (regional anaesthesia education)<xref rid="bib43" ref-type="bibr"><sup>43</sup></xref> (<xref rid="tbl1" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref>). Subquestions were then amalgamated into the following research question: &#x02018;What is the available evidence for the use of innovative technologies in regional anaesthesia education?&#x02019;</p></sec><sec id="sec2.4"><title>Inclusion criteria</title><p id="p0050">
<list list-type="simple" id="ulist0010"><list-item id="u0010"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0055">Intervention: use of innovative technologies (artificial intelligence [AI], augmented reality [AR] or virtual reality [VR]) as defined in <xref rid="tbl1" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref></p></list-item><list-item id="u0015"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0060">Outcome: any use of regional anaesthesia as defined in <xref rid="tbl1" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref></p></list-item><list-item id="u0020"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0065">Population: any anaesthetist or anaesthesiologist (includes those enrolled in a postgraducate medical training program) as defined in <xref rid="tbl1" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref></p></list-item><list-item id="u0025"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0070">Context: reference to regional anaesthesia education or training utility or contexts</p></list-item><list-item id="u0030"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0075">Study design: reports of quantitative, qualitative or mixed-methods studies of any design, including systematic reviews and grey literature</p></list-item><list-item id="u0035"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0080">Time: no limitations on publication date or duration of intervention</p></list-item></list>
</p></sec><sec id="sec2.5"><title>Exclusion criteria</title><p id="p0085">
<list list-type="simple" id="ulist0015"><list-item id="u0040"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0090">Inclusion criteria not met</p></list-item><list-item id="u0045"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0095">Full text unavailable in English</p></list-item><list-item id="u0050"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0100">Inadequate data reported (e.g. intervention, outcome or population not clearly defined)</p></list-item></list>
</p></sec><sec id="sec2.6"><title>Search strategy</title><p id="p0105">Three bibliographic databases were searched from inception to 5 May 2024; Medline, Scopus, and Web of Science. Databases were selected to ensure a broad return of studies from multidisciplinary fields, including both health and social sciences. Additional studies were identified by manual citation searches using reference lists from retrieved studies, until the point of data saturation. An initial scoping literature search was performed in Medline on 26 April 2024, which was superseded by the searches performed on May 5 2024. The search strategy was devised by the author in conjunction with University College London library academic services. The search comprised abstract and title keywords for the terms &#x02018;anaesthetist&#x02019;, &#x02018;regional anaesthesia&#x02019;, &#x02018;artificial intelligence&#x02019;, &#x02018;virtual reality&#x02019;, and &#x02018;augmented reality&#x02019;, with the use of synonyms and truncation symbols to ensure broad capture. No limiting filters were set. Results were imported into Endnote 20<xref rid="bib44" ref-type="bibr"><sup>44</sup></xref> for automated and then manual deduplication. Remaining citations were imported into Covidence<xref rid="bib45" ref-type="bibr"><sup>45</sup></xref> for further review.</p></sec><sec id="sec2.7"><title>Data collection</title><p id="p0110">After deduplication, titles and abstracts were screened against the defined inclusion criteria and 114 studies were retrieved for full-text review. A total of 38 studies were deemed eligible for final inclusion in this scoping review. A summary of the search process is shown in the PRISMA flow diagram provided in <xref rid="tbl2" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref>. Full narrative reasoning was recorded for all exclusion judgements to provide reflexive transparency in decision-making (<xref rid="appsec1" ref-type="sec">Supplementary Appendix 1</xref>).<table-wrap position="float" id="tbl2"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>PRISMA flow diagram. PRISMA 2020 flow diagram for new systematic reviews which included searches of databases and registers and alternative sources. PRISMA, Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses.</p></caption><alt-text id="alttext0030">Table 2</alt-text><graphic xlink:href="fx1"/><attrib>Source: Page MJ, and colleagues. <italic>BMJ</italic> 2021; 372:71, doi: 10.1136/bmj.n71. This work is licensed under CC BY 4.0. To view a copy of this license, visit <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0" id="intref0020">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0</ext-link></attrib></table-wrap></p></sec><sec id="sec2.8"><title>Data extraction</title><p id="p0115">Data were extracted and tabulated using a customised form which was piloted on the first five studies as per the methodological adaptions proposed by Levac and colleagues.<xref rid="bib38" ref-type="bibr"><sup>38</sup></xref> The returned data were considered against its ability to answer the aforementioned research questions, and the form was refined for the full data extraction process. The following datapoints were extracted: citation, author(s), year of publication, title, journal of publication, study design, topic/focus, type(s) of innovative technology studied, intervention and comparator (if any), study population or participants, outcome measures, conclusions related to educational utility.</p></sec><sec id="sec2.9"><title>Data analysis</title><p id="p0120">Data were analysed in two stages, in line with the scoping review conventions described in the study protocol. First numerical analysis was completed, focussing on key characteristics such as year of publication, populations studied, methodologies used, and focus of research. Next, reflexive thematic analysis was performed, utilising the approach proposed by Braun and Clark<xref rid="bib46" ref-type="bibr"><sup>46</sup></xref> and description by Byrne.<xref rid="bib47" ref-type="bibr"><sup>47</sup></xref> This consisted of familiarisation with data during the charting process, followed by semantic and latent coding. Codes were then refined based on frequency and content in an iterative and reflexive process, then refined into subthemes and subsequent themes.</p></sec></sec><sec id="sec3"><title>Results</title><p id="p0125">A range of innovative technologies were studied, with the majority classified as AI (48%), and the remaining consisting of VR (37%) and AR (15%) (<xref rid="tbl3" ref-type="table">Table 3</xref>). Included studies were published between the years of 1999 and 2024 and the majority of studies were published in the last 4 yr (<italic>n</italic>=25, 66%) in the distribution demonstrated in <xref rid="fig1" ref-type="fig">Figure 1</xref>. This distribution reflects both the novelty of innovative technologies, and their ever-increasing adoption in regional anaesthesia. A wide range of methodologies were also noted among the returned studies. The majority were classified as primary experimental research utilising designs such as prospective observational studies and external validation studies. Fourteen studies utilised review methodology, including three scoping reviews and two narrative reviews. The remaining studies consisted of randomized controlled trial (RCT) methodology, conference/symposium presentations and an MSc dissertation.<table-wrap position="float" id="tbl3"><label>Table 3</label><caption><p>Summary of studies included in scoping review. AI, artificial intelligence; AR augmented reality; SBME, simulation-based medical education; UGRA, ultrasound-guided regional anaesthesia; US, ultrasound; VR, virtual reality; VSG.</p></caption><alt-text id="alttext0035">Table 3</alt-text><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th>Citation</th><th>Type(s) of innovative technology studied</th><th>Educational utility and benefits</th><th>Limitations and risks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Balavenkatasubramanian et al., 2024<xref rid="bib50" ref-type="bibr"><sup>50</sup></xref></td><td>AI</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0020"><list-item id="u0055"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0215">Supervision for non-experts in UGRA can be provided by assistive AI</p></list-item><list-item id="u0060"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0220">Improved identification and recognition of sonoanatomy</p></list-item><list-item id="u0065"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0225">Encouraged independent practice</p></list-item><list-item id="u0070"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0230">Improved learner motivation</p></list-item><list-item id="u0075"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0235">Facilitates enhanced learn-ing in non-clinical settings</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0025"><list-item id="u0080"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0240">Software not validated in obese, paediatric, geriatric patients and can be inaccurate when there is complex anatomy</p></list-item><list-item id="u0085"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0245">Should be used for additional information (e.g. augmentation of images) rather than as a decision-maker</p></list-item><list-item id="u0090"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0250">Ethical and legal concerns, particularly surrounding data protection and accountability for errors</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Blezek et al., 2000<xref rid="bib73" ref-type="bibr"><sup>73</sup></xref></td><td>VR</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0030"><list-item id="u0095"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0255">Provision of reliable learn-ing experiences free from clinical risk</p></list-item><list-item id="u0100"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0260">Incorporated patient-specific anatomy allows for customisable learning experiences and rehearsal of procedures</p></list-item><list-item id="u0105"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0265">High interest in the inter-vention from surveyed population</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0035"><list-item id="u0110"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0270">System is currently under-going validation studies for educational usefulness</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Bogdanovych and Chuan, 2021<xref rid="bib74" ref-type="bibr"><sup>74</sup></xref></td><td>VR</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0040"><list-item id="u0115"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0275">Enhanced learner imm-ersion and interaction secondary to augmented simulation fidelity</p></list-item><list-item id="u0120"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0280">Elimination of distraction</p></list-item><list-item id="u0125"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0285">Improved learner motiv-ation secondary to novelty</p></list-item><list-item id="u0130"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0290">Enhanced recall secondary to the above phenomena</p></list-item><list-item id="u0135"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0295">Rehearsal of physical move-ments improves &#x02018;muscle memory&#x02019;</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0045"><list-item id="u0140"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0300">No experimental testing of the software is reported</p></list-item><list-item id="u0145"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0305">Clinical trials for the software delayed because of COVID restrictions</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Bowness et al., 2023<xref rid="bib54" ref-type="bibr"><sup>54</sup></xref></td><td>AI</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0050"><list-item id="u0150"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0310">AI assistive software for UGRA can improve confidence in non-expert operators, leading to increased operator independence and motivation</p></list-item><list-item id="u0155"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0315">Assistive AI may support a wider range of personnel in learning and performing UGRA</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0055"><list-item id="u0160"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0320">Study was not blinded because of feasibility</p></list-item></list>&#x02022;Time to perform blocks was virtually the same for AI-assisted blocks and non-assisted blocks</td></tr><tr><td>Bownesset al., 2022<xref rid="bib53" ref-type="bibr"><sup>53</sup></xref></td><td>AI</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0060"><list-item id="u0165"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0325">Positive feedback from users on a role in teaching and training Reported improved confidence in scanning and identification of sonoanatomy</p></list-item><list-item id="u0170"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0330">May enhance learning in non-clinical settings such as educational courses</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0065"><list-item id="u0175"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0335">Outcome data, including patient outcomes and resource-use metrics are still required to validate this device and software for clinical use in the long term</p></list-item><list-item id="u0180"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0340">Single-site study with a small sample size</p></list-item><list-item id="u0185"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0345">Increased supervisor inter-vention reported, authors posit this may be owing to the novelty of the technology</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Bowness et al., 2021<xref rid="bib52" ref-type="bibr"><sup>52</sup></xref></td><td>AI</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0070"><list-item id="u0190"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0350">AI technology may enhance accessibility and learning opportunities for clinicians and can provide consistency in US image acquisition and interpretation when performing UGRA</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0075"><list-item id="u0195"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0355">Software is not validated in paediatric or obese patients and those with abnormal or complex anatomy</p></list-item><list-item id="u0200"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0360">Risks clinician overreliance, particularly if the operator is less confident in their own competences</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Bowness et al., 2021<xref rid="bib52" ref-type="bibr"><sup>52</sup></xref></td><td>AI</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0080"><list-item id="u0205"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0365">Assistive AI can provide additional learning opportunities, including outside the clinical environment</p></list-item><list-item id="u0210"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0370">Accelerated learning and initial skill acquisition in sonoanatomy image interpretation</p></list-item><list-item id="u0215"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0375">Quantitative evaluation of user performance may have a role in the standardisation of assessment for UGRA</p></list-item><list-item id="u0220"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0380">Reliable AI technologies could optimise and standardise learning and training in UGRA mitigating inter-clinician variance</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0085"><list-item id="u0225"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0385">Few products have received regulatory approval for clinical use currently so the evidence is still limited for both clinical and educational outcomes</p></list-item><list-item id="u0230"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0390">Users must still learn decision-making and US probe handling to obtain the images for interpretation, skills which are not affected by AI image interpretation</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Bowness et al., 2024<xref rid="bib55" ref-type="bibr"><sup>55</sup></xref></td><td>AI</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0090"><list-item id="u0235"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0395">Non-experts in UGRA deem assistive AI systems useful in their training and experts reported them useful in teaching</p></list-item><list-item id="u0240"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0400">Higher self-rating scores among anaesthetic trainees reported when practicing UGRA with AI assistance</p></list-item><list-item id="u0245"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0405">Reduced paraesthesia during the first month of performing sciatic nerve blocks after teaching with an AI assistive system</p></list-item><list-item id="u0250"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0410">Reduced needle pass atte-mpts, time taken to perform regional anaesthesia, and increased performance and delivery of regional anaesthesia</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0095"><list-item id="u0255"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0415">Significant heterogeneity and poor reporting across the literature base</p></list-item><list-item id="u0260"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0420">Lack of evidence pertaining to AI in UGRA which includes educational utility as either primary or secondary outcome measures</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Cai et al., 2022<xref rid="bib56" ref-type="bibr"><sup>56</sup></xref></td><td>AI</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0100"><list-item id="u0265"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0425">Lower complication rates of paraesthesia and pain during injection</p></list-item><list-item id="u0270"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0430">Higher Assessment Checklist scores in AI teaching group</p></list-item><list-item id="u0275"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0435">Emphasising the anatomical features in ultrasound images (using AI) can shorten the time needed for trainees to learn and master the relevant knowledge</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0105"><list-item id="u0280"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0440">Focussed on the popliteal sciatic block which is relatively easy to identify for novices thus AI assistance may have a larger role in blocks with more difficult image interpretation</p></list-item><list-item id="u0285"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0445">Acquisition of higher order skills such as clinical decision-making were not investigated</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Charissis et al., 2010<xref rid="bib66" ref-type="bibr"><sup>66</sup></xref></td><td>VR, AR</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0110"><list-item id="u0290"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0450">Learning can be augmented through the use of realistic 3D human anatomy models and human computer interaction</p></list-item><list-item id="u0295"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0455">Virtual learning environments can be used outside the clinical environment, free from clinical risk, and can be projected on a screen for larger-scale classroom teaching</p></list-item><list-item id="u0300"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0460">Can improve trainee fam-iliarisation with the 3D anatomy and 2D sonoanatomy for brachial plexus blocks, potentially improving confidence before performing these procedures in clinical settings</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0115"><list-item id="u0305"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0465">This system does not offer the user feedback: either visual auditory or haptic</p></list-item><list-item id="u0310"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0470">The fidelity of this system may be limited by the user controls being via a computer keyboard and joystick and authors suggest future efforts might aim to include realistic operating tools such as an US probe, regional anaesthesia needle/kit, and phantom limb</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Chuan et al., 2023<xref rid="bib32" ref-type="bibr"><sup>32</sup></xref></td><td>VR</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0120"><list-item id="u0315"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0475">Demonstration of construct validity for a bespoke VR system used to teach regional anaesthesia, providing personalised learning curves</p></list-item><list-item id="u0320"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0480">Accelerated learning and enhanced learner exposure to complex skills before practise in clinical settings</p></list-item><list-item id="u0325"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0485">Advantages over traditional methods of UGRA education such durability, scalability, opportunity for remote learning, and cost-effectiveness</p></list-item><list-item id="u0330"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0490">Software upgrades could incorporate principles from gamification such as rewards or friendly competition to improve learner motivation</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0125"><list-item id="u0335"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0495">Convenience sampling size of 15 participants for each arm with larger RCT recommended</p></list-item><list-item id="u0340"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0500">Cybersickness not measured but identified as relevant</p></list-item><list-item id="u0345"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0505">Experienced users reported lower levels of immersion suggesting VR simulation may have optimum educational outcomes when utilised with non-experts</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Deserno et al., 2015<xref rid="bib67" ref-type="bibr"><sup>67</sup></xref></td><td>VR, AR</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0130"><list-item id="u0350"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0510">Enhanced access to learning opportunities with diverse and specific simulated patients</p></list-item><list-item id="u0355"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0515">Imported patient-specific datasets allow rehearsal of regional anaesthesia procedures, improving confidence and performance</p></list-item><list-item id="u0360"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0520">Cost effective when com-pared against conventional methods of simulated regional anaesthesia training</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0135"><list-item id="u0365"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0525">The system described has not been validated or incorporated into normal clinical practice</p></list-item><list-item id="u0370"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0530">Logistical and data gover-nance issues may arise in performing, storing, and importing real patient scans to this system</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Grottke et al., 2009<xref rid="bib80" ref-type="bibr"><sup>80</sup></xref></td><td>VR</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0140"><list-item id="u0375"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0535">Advantages over conventional manikin training for regional anaesthesia education such as provision of improved representation of biological tissue, inter-patient variability and mitigation of physical wear because of repeated use</p></list-item><list-item id="u0380"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0540">Can reliably represent a range of patients and regional anaesthesia procedures and imitate specific sterical relationships, enhancing learning</p></list-item><list-item id="u0385"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0545">Improved learner confidence secondary to practise in a setting free from clinical risk</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0145"><list-item id="u0390"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0550">No formal evaluation repo-rted and trainee acceptability, satisfaction, and learning outcomes are yet to be tested</p></list-item><list-item id="u0395"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0555">The VR system, though novel at the time of publication, is technologically rudimental by contemporary comparison</p></list-item><list-item id="u0400"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0560">Only one regional ana-esthesia procedure simulated (inguinal nerve block), however authors propose this could be expanded to others</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Gungor et al., 2023<xref rid="bib58" ref-type="bibr"><sup>58</sup></xref></td><td>AI</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0150"><list-item id="u0405"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0565">Augmented education and training through supported US scanning, enhanced image acquisition, and interpretation</p></list-item><list-item id="u0410"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0570">Optimised block views, particularly in deeply located blocks (e.g. sciatic nerve block or quadratus lumborum block)</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0155"><list-item id="u0415"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0575">System not validated in obese patients, however, this validation study did not find any statistical difference when the system was used for patients with a range of BMIs</p></list-item><list-item id="u0420"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0580">Exclusion criteria included patients with complex or abnormal anatomy or previous surgery in the studied anatomical regions, despite this population representing greater procedural difficulty with higher potential utility for these technologies</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Gungor et al., 2021<xref rid="bib57" ref-type="bibr"><sup>57</sup></xref></td><td>AI</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0160"><list-item id="u0425"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0585">AI-assisted US scanning for UGRA improves training for non-experts and performance of experts Embedded electronic tutorials were particularly helpful for novice users in identifying key anatomical structures</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0165"><list-item id="u0430"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0590">Paediatric, geriatric, and patients with complex or difficult anatomy were not represented in this study</p></list-item><list-item id="u0435"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0595">Only four blocks (chosen as frequently performed in authors institution) were evaluated</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Gupta et al., 2023<xref rid="bib59" ref-type="bibr"><sup>59</sup></xref></td><td>AI</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0170"><list-item id="u0440"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0600">AI assistive software has been demonstrated to enhance training in non-specialists and provide instruction for specialists when performing UGRA</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0175"><list-item id="u0445"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0605">Broad review article lacking specific focus on the use of novel technologies in UGRA training and education</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Harutyunyan et al., 2024<xref rid="bib60" ref-type="bibr"><sup>60</sup></xref></td><td>AI, VR, AR</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0180"><list-item id="u0450"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0610">AI can enhance education and training through augmented US image recognition and provision of user guidance and real-time feedback</p></list-item><list-item id="u0455"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0615">Future developments such as AR and VR technologies (for example, holograms) could further enhance education through the above mechanisms with enhanced fidelity</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0185"><list-item id="u0460"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0620">Primary evidence is not reported, with authors acknowledging the infancy of the field and calling for further research</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Hewson and Bedforth, 2023<xref rid="bib10" ref-type="bibr"><sup>10</sup></xref></td><td>AI</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0190"><list-item id="u0465"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0625">Requirements for UGRA competency are increasing and efficiency for training and lifelong learning need to increase to achieve this</p></list-item><list-item id="u0470"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0630">AI assistive software can mitigate direct supervision requirements and non-experts in UGRA reported higher perceived educational utility for AI assistive software than experts</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0195"><list-item id="u0475"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0635">The studies referenced were small scale (lacking power calculations and assessor blinding) which may affect validity</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Huang et al., 2020<xref rid="bib78" ref-type="bibr"><sup>78</sup></xref></td><td>VR</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0200"><list-item id="u0480"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0640">A computer screen-based VR system for learning USG interscalene block was shown to improve learning in moderately experienced trainees (without a significant effect on inexperienced or very experienced trainees)</p></list-item><list-item id="u0485"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0645">An interactive virtual 3D model of the lumbar spine related to UGRA was found to improve knowledge scores among anaesthesia trainees</p></list-item><list-item id="u0490"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0650">VR simulator for performing UGRA procedures offers higher spatial fidelity than conventional manikin simulators</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0205"><list-item id="u0495"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0655">Construct validation studies have not been reported for the studied interventions</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Jo et al., 2023<xref rid="bib61" ref-type="bibr"><sup>61</sup></xref></td><td>AI</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0210"><list-item id="u0500"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0660">Similar interventions have been shown to support non-experts in learning, and experts in teaching UGRA procedures</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0215"><list-item id="u0505"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0665">Clinical efficacy for the computer diagnosis systems tested have not yet been demonstrated, authors suggest further research with a focus on quantitative outcomes</p></list-item><list-item id="u0510"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0670">Single-centre study and au-thors recommend a larger-scale multicentre trial to improve validity of findings</p></list-item><list-item id="u0515"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0675">Authors do not present prim-ary data on educational outcomes but report findings from similar studies related to educational utility</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Kim et al., 2023<xref rid="bib75" ref-type="bibr"><sup>75</sup></xref></td><td>VR</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0220"><list-item id="u0520"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0680">Higher satisfaction and technical proficiency scores reported in the intervention group</p></list-item><list-item id="u0525"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0685">Advantages over conven-tional cadaver training include less faculty requirements and reduced overcrowding, which authors suggest is beneficial in the post-pandemic context. Other advantages include reduced radiation exposure (for this specific procedure) and provision of reliable, repeatable learning opportunities</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0225"><list-item id="u0530"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0690">Authors suggest haptic feedback could improve simulator fidelity to maximise effectiveness</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Tulgar et al., 2023<xref rid="bib48" ref-type="bibr"><sup>48</sup></xref></td><td>AI</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0230"><list-item id="u0535"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0695">The majority of participants (&#x0003e;70%) believed that the use of AI in regional anaesthesia training will benefit both trainers and trainees, and contribute to equal opportunity in medical education</p></list-item><list-item id="u0540"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0700">The majority of participants believed that supporting regional anaesthesia training with AI would lessen problems in both resident and postgraduate anaesthesiology training</p></list-item><list-item id="u0545"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0705">The population studied showed willingness to engage with this technology</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0235"><list-item id="u0550"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0710">Authors do not posit mech-anisms by which AI can enhance regional anaesthesia training in anaesthesiology as that was outside the scope of this study</p></list-item><list-item id="u0555"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0715">Notable ethical issues of responsibility, error, and data governance in using AI for regional anaesthesia procedures and training</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Lim et al., 2005<xref rid="bib79" ref-type="bibr"><sup>79</sup></xref></td><td>VR</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0240"><list-item id="u0560"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0720">Improved reported under-standing of both anatomical and technical principles of the interscalene block</p></list-item><list-item id="u0565"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0725">Some 24.1% measured im-provement in anatomical knowledge post intervention</p></list-item><list-item id="u0570"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0730">VR technologies allow de-monstration of spatial relationships in regional anaesthesia anatomy education, which would not be achievable with other teaching modalities</p></list-item><list-item id="u0575"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0735">Highest educational utility in part-task training, providing trainees with prerequisite knowledge and confidence before learning in clinical environments</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0245"><list-item id="u0580"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0740">The group with highest prior experience in interscalene blocks demonstrated a reduction in anatomical knowledge on the photo-survey exercise post intervention</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Martin et al., 1999<xref rid="bib76" ref-type="bibr"><sup>76</sup></xref></td><td>VR</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0250"><list-item id="u0585"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0745">VR learning allows practise in a setting free from clinical risk, reducing anxiety in trainees and trainers. It also creates consistent educational opportunities, addressing a barrier to learning associated with traditional methods of regional anaesthesia education</p></list-item><list-item id="u0590"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0750">Patient anatomic inform-ation can be integrated allowing targeted learning and rehearsal of difficult procedures, which may lead to more rapid learning</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0255"><list-item id="u0595"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0755">Authors present the de-velopment of a VR simulator but do not report any primary evaluative data</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>McKendrick et al., 2021<xref rid="bib8" ref-type="bibr"><sup>8</sup></xref></td><td>AI, AR, VR</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0260"><list-item id="u0600"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0760">AI can improve anatomical navigation in UGRA which may improve learning</p></list-item><list-item id="u0605"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0765">AR augmented regional an-aesthesia training has advantages over traditional methods of learning such a cadaveric training, which is associated with poor accessibility and high costs which limit its use</p></list-item><list-item id="u0610"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0770">VR has been demonstrated to augment learning in epidural anaesthesia through enhanced learner motivation through gamification</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0265"><list-item id="u0615"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0775">This review article presents the history and scope of utilisation of AI and robotics in regional anaesthesia but presents minimal primary data related to educational outcomes</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Niazi et al., 2016<xref rid="bib81" ref-type="bibr"><sup>81</sup></xref></td><td>VR</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0270"><list-item id="u0620"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0780">VR simulation has been shown to improve knowledge and technique before practise in the clinical setting</p></list-item><list-item id="u0625"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0785">A study evaluating a VR simulator for lumbar spine regional anaesthesia demonstrated that after 2 weeks, anaesthesia trainees who had full access to the VR simulator were able to identify double the key anatomical landmarks on a simulated patient than those who had partial access to the simulator</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0275"><list-item id="u0630"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0790">This is a comprehensive narrative review of concepts that could be applied from surgical skills training to UGRA training, but authors only make minimal reference to innovative technologies (as defined by the inclusion criteria of this scoping review)</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>O&#x02019;Sullivan et al., 2014<xref rid="bib82" ref-type="bibr"><sup>82</sup></xref></td><td>VR</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0280"><list-item id="u0635"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0795">Distributed practice is more effective than massed practice and VR simulators are a route to achieving this</p></list-item><list-item id="u0640"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0800">VR simulators allow learni-ng at a pace that is determined by the individual and may reduce the need for direct supervision, moving learners towards self-regulated deliberate study</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0285"><list-item id="u0645"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0805">Because of early termination of this study, the authors present a limited sample size which affects validity and generalisability of results</p></list-item><list-item id="u0650"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0810">Authors found their VR simulator prototype to be insufficient to meet their training demands and contemplate that unvalidated simulators could lead to incorrect or dangerous practices if used by learners in an unsupervised environment</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Philippona, 2014<xref rid="bib62" ref-type="bibr"><sup>62</sup></xref></td><td>AI</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0290"><list-item id="u0655"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0815">AI assistance in regional anaesthesia can provide peer-to-peer learning opportunities and reduce requirements for expert supervision</p></list-item><list-item id="u0660"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0820">The software can act as a &#x02018;second opinion&#x02019; encouraging self-directed learning in the clinical setting</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0295"><list-item id="u0665"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0825">Authors suggest larger evalu-ations are required to improve the validity of findings</p></list-item><list-item id="u0670"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0830">The software described was developed for two nerve blocks: the femoral and popliteal, limiting generalisability</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Ramlogan et al., 2017)<xref rid="bib49" ref-type="bibr"><sup>49</sup></xref></td><td>VR</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0300"><list-item id="u0675"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0835">Trainees demonstrated a statistically significant improvement (nearly 40%) on their test scores post intervention and questionnaire data demonstrated the trainees found the tool valuable and easy to use</p></list-item><list-item id="u0680"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0840">VR learning provides non-static visual representations of relevant anatomy and sonoanatomy that can be manipulated by the user</p></list-item><list-item id="u0685"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0845">Proposed high utility in part-task training before transition to clinical settings</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0305"><list-item id="u0690"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0850">No control group used as authors propose the intervention is an additional educational modality rather than a substitution for conventional methods of instruction</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Ramlogan et al., 2021<xref rid="bib35" ref-type="bibr"><sup>35</sup></xref></td><td>AI, AR, VR</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0310"><list-item id="u0695"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0855">AR systems can decrease quality-compromising manoeuvres in novices performing regional anaesthesia (such as head tilt and probe sliding)</p></list-item><list-item id="u0700"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0860">Eye-tracking technologies can distinguish between experts and novices and could be used to assess competencies and effectiveness of teaching</p></list-item><list-item id="u0705"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0865">AI assistance could provide support in performing regional anaesthesia procedures, encouraging deliberate self-guided practice with reduced supervisory requirements</p></list-item><list-item id="u0710"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0870">AR/VR systems have been shown to be engaging and improve learner motivation</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0315"><list-item id="u0715"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0875">Innovative technologies may be associated with prohibitive initial costs, lack of feedback (e.g. realistic haptic feedback in VR tools) and lack of holistic training outside of the procedural task (e.g. how to proceed in a patient with difficult anatomy or how to manage periprocedural patient pain or discomfort)</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Sardesai et al., 2024<xref rid="bib83" ref-type="bibr"><sup>83</sup></xref></td><td>AI</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0320"><list-item id="u0720"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0880">Users rated the intervention highly for intuitiveness, accuracy (replicating real life), and feasibility (replacing actors in assessments)</p></list-item><list-item id="u0725"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0885">Other studies demonstrate high levels of trainee satisfaction, enjoyment, and confidence after using educational chatbots and improved assessment scores</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0325"><list-item id="u0730"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0890">Issues related to chatbot realism such as an overly formal, repetitive or friendly manner, lacking the nuances of real-life patient interactions</p></list-item><list-item id="u0735"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0895">Ethical issues related to copyright and transparency</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Scholten et al., 2017<xref rid="bib68" ref-type="bibr"><sup>68</sup></xref></td><td>AR</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0330"><list-item id="u0740"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0900">AR techniques have shown excellent potential when used for educational purposes</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0335"><list-item id="u0745"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0905">Authors discuss multiple technologies but only AR meets the inclusion criteria for this scoping review</p></list-item><list-item id="u0750"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0910">No specific mechanisms proposed for augmented learning</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Shevlin et al., 2023<xref rid="bib69" ref-type="bibr"><sup>69</sup></xref></td><td>AR</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0340"><list-item id="u0755"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0915">This device offers a high fidelity AR simulator as an alternative to the traditional methods of needle-tip orientation skill acquisition, which are associated with limited training opportunities</p></list-item><list-item id="u0760"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0920">Data collected by this system could have a role in assessment</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0345"><list-item id="u0765"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0925">Assessors were unblinded for assessing the studies&#x02019; secondary outcomes and authors acknowledge a relatively small sample size</p></list-item><list-item id="u0770"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0930">Primary aims of this study were to evaluate whether the intervention could distinguish between non-expert and expert regional anaesthesia practitioners, not educational utility</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Smistad et al., 2018<xref rid="bib63" ref-type="bibr"><sup>63</sup></xref></td><td>AI</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0350"><list-item id="u0775"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0935">Highlighting key anatomical structures in real time could augment trainee learning for inexperienced practitioners of UGRA</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0355"><list-item id="u0780"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0940">Limited accuracy and preci-sion of the software which could be improved with incorporation of further datasets</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Sujatta, 2015<xref rid="bib77" ref-type="bibr"><sup>77</sup></xref></td><td>VR</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0360"><list-item id="u0785"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0945">VR simulation offers reliable and repeatable learning opportunities, free from clinical risk</p></list-item><list-item id="u0790"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0950">VR can significantly enhance fidelity in SBME</p></list-item><list-item id="u0795"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0955">Authors concluded that no extendable software-based VR simulator for UGRA exists and suggest that the systems included in the review required significant preparation, time, and manpower to set up, often available in feasibility studies but not afforded in clinical training contexts</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0365"><list-item id="u0800"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0960">Traditionally VR simulators in regional anaesthesia are associated with low learner acceptance because of limited realism, though this is mitigated by technological advancements</p></list-item><list-item id="u0805"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0965">There has been increased development and evaluation of VR systems used for UGRA training since the publication of this review</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Viderman et al., 2022<xref rid="bib31" ref-type="bibr"><sup>31</sup></xref></td><td>AI</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0370"><list-item id="u0810"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0970">Robust AI-assisted technologies can improve clinician performance and training in UGRA particularly among non-experts</p></list-item><list-item id="u0815"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0975">Increased adoption of innovative technologies will have a large educational impact and anaesthesia providers should be involved in this transformation</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0375"><list-item id="u0820"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0980">There is a significant lack of RCTs and large-scale studies in the associated literature</p></list-item><list-item id="u0825"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0985">AI assistive technologies may transform training in non-clinical settings, but the importance of clinical practise remains fundamental</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Wu and Wang, 2021<xref rid="bib64" ref-type="bibr"><sup>64</sup></xref></td><td>AI</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0380"><list-item id="u0830"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0990">Recognition of anatomy can be difficult in obese, older, and trauma patients because of altered anatomy. AI systems have been shown to improve recognition of key anatomical structures, particularly in complex cases</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0385"><list-item id="u0835"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p0995">The primary evidence cited relates to AI systems used to characterise liver fibrosis and thyroid nodules Authors do not cite specific examples related to UGRA training or education</p></list-item></list></td></tr><tr><td>Xu et al., 2023<xref rid="bib65" ref-type="bibr"><sup>65</sup></xref></td><td>AI</td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0390"><list-item id="u0840"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p1000">Training opportunities in UGRA are limited by patient caseload and safety considerations, necessitating accelerated skill acquisition</p></list-item><list-item id="u0845"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p1005">The system showed high accuracy scores and provides real-time quantitative feedback which learners can use to drive rapid improvement</p></list-item><list-item id="u0850"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p1010">Reduced supervisory re-quirements facilitates self-directed deliberate practise in a low-risk context, requiring minimal cost and manpower</p></list-item></list></td><td><list list-type="simple" id="ulist0395"><list-item id="u0855"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p1015">Authors did not describe how &#x02018;ground truth&#x02019; was determined during assessment of the intervention</p></list-item><list-item id="u0860"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p1020">Findings were related to practise of UGRA skills on a phantom limb but authors propose future work including trialling the intervention on cadaveric specimens, healthy volunteers or patients</p></list-item><list-item id="u0865"><label>&#x02022;</label><p id="p1025">Further work is needed focussing on specific educational outcomes, alongside qualitative evaluative data</p></list-item></list></td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><fig id="fig1"><label>Fig 1</label><caption><p>Distribution of studies by year of publication.</p></caption><alt-text id="alttext0015">Fig 1</alt-text><graphic xlink:href="gr1"/></fig></p><sec id="sec3.1"><title>Themes</title><p id="p0130">The themes generated via reflexive thematic analysis were organised into a thematic map, demonstrated in <xref rid="fig2" ref-type="fig">Figure 2</xref>. Five main themes were identified for further analysis.<fig id="fig2"><label>Fig 2</label><caption><p>Thematic map. SBME, simulation-based medical education; UGRA, ultrasound-guided regional anaesthesia.</p></caption><alt-text id="alttext0020">Fig 2</alt-text><graphic xlink:href="gr2"/></fig></p><sec id="sec3.1.1"><title>Supporting ultrasound-guided regional anaesthesia procedural skill acquisition</title><p id="p0135">Several papers described the use of AI assistive technology in performing US-guided regional anaesthesia (UGRA) procedures.<xref rid="bib4" ref-type="bibr"><sup>4</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib8" ref-type="bibr"><sup>8</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib10" ref-type="bibr"><sup>10</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib31" ref-type="bibr"><sup>31</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib35" ref-type="bibr"><sup>35</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib50" ref-type="bibr">50</xref>, <xref rid="bib51" ref-type="bibr">51</xref>, <xref rid="bib52" ref-type="bibr">52</xref>, <xref rid="bib53" ref-type="bibr">53</xref>, <xref rid="bib54" ref-type="bibr">54</xref>, <xref rid="bib55" ref-type="bibr">55</xref>, <xref rid="bib56" ref-type="bibr">56</xref>, <xref rid="bib57" ref-type="bibr">57</xref>, <xref rid="bib58" ref-type="bibr">58</xref>, <xref rid="bib59" ref-type="bibr">59</xref>, <xref rid="bib60" ref-type="bibr">60</xref>, <xref rid="bib61" ref-type="bibr">61</xref>, <xref rid="bib62" ref-type="bibr">62</xref>, <xref rid="bib63" ref-type="bibr">63</xref>, <xref rid="bib64" ref-type="bibr">64</xref>, <xref rid="bib65" ref-type="bibr">65</xref> Key benefits of these systems related to augmenting acquisition and interpretation of US images. The majority of these technologies used machine learning, trained through imported datasets and validated by human experts in regional anaesthesia, to identify and highlight key anatomical structures (such as vasculature, muscles, and nerves) on the US machine during scanning. Multiple studies demonstrated that maximal benefits were observed in non-expert users, and included: improved user confidence, procedural success, and accelerated learning.<xref rid="bib31" ref-type="bibr"><sup>31</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib52" ref-type="bibr">52</xref>, <xref rid="bib53" ref-type="bibr">53</xref>, <xref rid="bib54" ref-type="bibr">54</xref>, <xref rid="bib55" ref-type="bibr">55</xref>, <xref rid="bib56" ref-type="bibr">56</xref>, <xref rid="bib57" ref-type="bibr">57</xref>, <xref rid="bib58" ref-type="bibr">58</xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib65" ref-type="bibr"><sup>65</sup></xref> Other studies suggested that this technology may reduce requirements for direct expert supervision during regional anaesthesia procedures, encouraging deliberate self-practice in the clinical setting.<xref rid="bib10" ref-type="bibr"><sup>10</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib35" ref-type="bibr"><sup>35</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib50" ref-type="bibr"><sup>50</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib62" ref-type="bibr"><sup>62</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib65" ref-type="bibr"><sup>65</sup></xref> Some highlighted the use of AR technology in supporting regional anaesthesia performance and described the use of three-dimensional spatial markers, projected into the users vision to assist in procedural navigation.<xref rid="bib8" ref-type="bibr"><sup>8</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib35" ref-type="bibr"><sup>35</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib60" ref-type="bibr"><sup>60</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib66" ref-type="bibr">66</xref>, <xref rid="bib67" ref-type="bibr">67</xref>, <xref rid="bib68" ref-type="bibr">68</xref>, <xref rid="bib69" ref-type="bibr">69</xref> AR techniques were posited to have high educational potential, particularly in supporting learners to develop needle handling skills, however this effect has yet to be robustly demonstrated in the literature.<xref rid="bib8" ref-type="bibr"><sup>8</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib68" ref-type="bibr"><sup>68</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib69" ref-type="bibr"><sup>69</sup></xref> Development of complementary technologies, such as eye tracking and holograms, were predicted to enhance the impact of AR within medical education in the future.<xref rid="bib35" ref-type="bibr"><sup>35</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib60" ref-type="bibr"><sup>60</sup></xref></p></sec><sec id="sec3.1.2"><title>Simulation-based medical education</title><p id="p0140">Simulation-based medical education (SBME) is a common practice in healthcare education utilising simulated patients to provide learners with a clinically safe environment to practise skills.<xref rid="bib70" ref-type="bibr"><sup>70</sup></xref> Patients can be simulated with actors, manikins, or in VR, with resultant effects on simulation fidelity. When a simulated experience closely reflects real life, it is considered to be high fidelity. Optimum fidelity in SBME remains a contested topic in the literature, however several studies have linked high fidelity experiences with increased transfer of learning.<xref rid="bib71" ref-type="bibr"><sup>71</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib72" ref-type="bibr"><sup>72</sup></xref> Innovative technologies were shown to augment psychological fidelity in SBME through increased user immersion and engagement, and multiple studies demonstrated improved learner confidence and knowledge associated with VR-enhanced SBME.<xref rid="bib32" ref-type="bibr"><sup>32</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib35" ref-type="bibr"><sup>35</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib73" ref-type="bibr">73</xref>, <xref rid="bib74" ref-type="bibr">74</xref>, <xref rid="bib75" ref-type="bibr">75</xref>, <xref rid="bib76" ref-type="bibr">76</xref>, <xref rid="bib77" ref-type="bibr">77</xref> Most studies citing the use of VR in this review referred to the use of simulated virtual learning environments, accessed through technology such as headsets, glasses, and gloves. Key benefits of VR simulators included provision of reliable, repeatable, and customisable learning opportunities, free from clinical risk.<xref rid="bib32" ref-type="bibr"><sup>32</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib67" ref-type="bibr"><sup>67</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib75" ref-type="bibr">75</xref>, <xref rid="bib76" ref-type="bibr">76</xref>, <xref rid="bib77" ref-type="bibr">77</xref> This addressed established barriers to learning associated with traditional methods of regional anaesthesia education.<xref rid="bib10" ref-type="bibr"><sup>10</sup></xref></p></sec></sec><sec id="sec3.2"><title>Enhanced anatomy and sonoanatomy education</title><p id="p0145">Multiple papers highlighted the advantages offered by innovative technologies for anatomy and sonoanatomy education, compared with traditional methods of instruction. One key benefit was the ability to represent spatial relationships using VR and AR learning tools.<xref rid="bib78" ref-type="bibr">78</xref>, <xref rid="bib79" ref-type="bibr">79</xref>, <xref rid="bib80" ref-type="bibr">80</xref> VR models allowed learners to manipulate models in three dimensions, customise displays to highlight key structures, or include specific layers such as skin, vasculature, and nerves, and certain studies demonstrated improved learning with these techniques.<xref rid="bib60" ref-type="bibr"><sup>60</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib66" ref-type="bibr"><sup>66</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib67" ref-type="bibr"><sup>67</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib79" ref-type="bibr">79</xref>, <xref rid="bib80" ref-type="bibr">80</xref>, <xref rid="bib81" ref-type="bibr">81</xref> Appreciation for anatomical sterical relationships were traditionally taught using two-dimensional representations or with cadaveric prosections, however limited access to this mode of teaching has been highlighted as a barrier to learning. AI technologies were also demonstrated to accelerate learning in this area, through identification and highlighting of key structures during US scanning. Several authors suggested that AI assistance could facilitate US scanning of peers or healthy volunteers, encouraging self-paced and self-directed learning and smoothing the transition to practice in the clinical setting.<xref rid="bib62" ref-type="bibr"><sup>62</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib82" ref-type="bibr"><sup>82</sup></xref></p><sec id="sec3.2.1"><title>Assessment</title><p id="p0150">Multiple studies highlighted a potential role for innovative technologies in future assessment practices, but emphasised the need for further validation studies before this can become standard. AI technologies were suggested to have two key roles. Certain AI assistive systems collected data during performance of regional anaesthesia procedures such as: time taken, adequacy of views obtained, and likelihood of procedural success.<xref rid="bib60" ref-type="bibr"><sup>60</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib65" ref-type="bibr"><sup>65</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib66" ref-type="bibr"><sup>66</sup></xref> These metrics were recommended to be used in formative assessment to drive further learning, however they could also be used to determine user competence within summative assessments. One study evaluated an AI chatbot that was used to simulate a patient requiring an regional anaesthesia procedure.<xref rid="bib83" ref-type="bibr"><sup>83</sup></xref> This was used to train users in discussing regional anaesthesia procedures with patients including benefits, risks, obtaining consent, and answering questions. Authors suggested that this technology could replace actors in future practical examinations, however they also highlighted issues such as realism, ethics, and acceptability.</p><p id="p0155">VR simulated learning environments were also theorised to have a potential role in assessment. During practise of regional anaesthesia procedures using VR technology, data such as number of attempts, needling precision, time taken, and block success rates were collected.<xref rid="bib8" ref-type="bibr"><sup>8</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib31" ref-type="bibr"><sup>31</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib60" ref-type="bibr"><sup>60</sup></xref> Authors suggested that these data could be used similarly to those collected by AI assistive technologies to determine the users competencies in both formative and summative assessments. Several authors posited that VR learning environments, because of their absence of clinical risk, could have high utility in determining when learners have achieved the competencies required to transition to practice in a clinical setting.<xref rid="bib35" ref-type="bibr"><sup>35</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib66" ref-type="bibr"><sup>66</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib76" ref-type="bibr"><sup>76</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib79" ref-type="bibr"><sup>79</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib80" ref-type="bibr"><sup>80</sup></xref></p></sec><sec id="sec3.2.2"><title>Further work needed</title><p id="p0160">The use of innovative technologies in medical education is a burgeoning field with predicted exponential growth, however their adoption in regional anaesthesia education remains in its infancy.<xref rid="bib29" ref-type="bibr"><sup>29</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib31" ref-type="bibr"><sup>31</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib60" ref-type="bibr"><sup>60</sup></xref> Consequently, much of the primary evidence included in this scoping review were small-scale, single-site studies recommending further larger-scale multicentre RCTs to validate findings.<xref rid="bib31" ref-type="bibr"><sup>31</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib36" ref-type="bibr"><sup>36</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib61" ref-type="bibr"><sup>61</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib62" ref-type="bibr"><sup>62</sup></xref> As a result, significant heterogeneity and overall poor reporting has been identified within the existing evidence base.<xref rid="bib55" ref-type="bibr"><sup>55</sup></xref> This scoping review also identified a predominance towards clinical outcomes in the literature base, with few studies reporting on specific educational outcomes and none adopting these as primary outcome measures.</p></sec></sec></sec><sec id="sec4"><title>Discussion</title><p id="p0165">This scoping review identified several processes through which innovative technologies contributed positively to regional anaesthesia training, with roles identified in; simulation-based medical education, preclinical education, clinical education, skill acquisition, and assessment. Two key mechanisms in which these technologies can best augment education (access to learning and supported supervision) are proposed, underpinned by core educational theories.</p><sec id="sec4.1"><title>Access to learning</title><p id="p0170">A central mechanism through which innovative technologies were found to augment learning in regional anaesthesia was through the provision of reliable educational experiences. Access to regional anaesthesia education has traditionally been dictated by the alignment of patient caseload, clinical risk, and availability of expert trainers.<xref rid="bib10" ref-type="bibr"><sup>10</sup></xref> Consequently, studies have demonstrated regional anaesthesia education to be inconsistent, variable, and <italic>ad hoc</italic> in its delivery.<xref rid="bib12" ref-type="bibr"><sup>12</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib35" ref-type="bibr"><sup>35</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib51" ref-type="bibr"><sup>51</sup></xref> VR learning environments offer repeatable, consistent, and customisable learning opportunities, in a setting free from clinical risk. These environments were shown to have positive effects on learner confidence, knowledge, and motivation, with potential roles in assessment and determination of learner competencies required for clinical practice. AI assistive systems were also shown to support learners in self-directed regional anaesthesia practice in both preclinical (with peers and healthy volunteers) and clinical (with patients) contexts. This phenomenon can be explored through core educational theories such as experiential learning.<xref rid="bib84" ref-type="bibr"><sup>84</sup></xref> Improved access to educational opportunities can provide learners with increased &#x02018;concrete experiences&#x02019; in which they can practise procedural skills, construct meaning, and develop understanding. When observing this phenomenon through the lens of Kolb&#x02019;s experiential learning cycle, the critical role of reflection upon these concrete experiences should be emphasised, as it is through this process that learners relate experiences to their current knowledge, synthesise new information, and create learning.<xref rid="bib85" ref-type="bibr"><sup>85</sup></xref></p></sec><sec id="sec4.2"><title>Supporting supervision</title><p id="p0175">Multiple papers in this review suggested that AI assistive systems could reduce supervisory requirements and empower learners to engage in self-guided practice when learning regional anaesthesia skills. This effect was observed to be maximal among non-experts. Traditionally, learners practicing in the clinical setting require direct supervision, creating an additional barrier to accessing leaning opportunities. AI assistive technology was shown to provide clinicians with a &#x02018;second opinion,&#x02019; aiding users in obtaining optimal US views and confirming their assumptions in interpreting US images. Through these actions, AI could meet some of the traditional &#x02018;supervisory&#x02019; requirements, particularly in the cohort of non-experts who have completed multiple regional anaesthesia procedures. Observing this phenomenon through the lens of social constructivism, these learners could be considered to be operating in the zone of proximal development.<xref rid="bib86" ref-type="bibr"><sup>86</sup></xref> This theory represents tasks beyond a learners current expertise, and proposes that learners can develop skill mastery through experimentation with external instruction and guided support. As learners progress, their supervisory requirements decrease and these feasibly might be met by AI assistive systems. Innovative technologies are predicted to become increasingly integrated into healthcare education, and if they can reduce requirements for direct supervision whilst learning regional anaesthesia, learners can be empowered to seek additional learning opportunities that were previously inaccessible. A scoping review conducted by Savage and colleagues<xref rid="bib87" ref-type="bibr"><sup>87</sup></xref> substantiates these findings, though as it was published after this scoping review was designed and conducted, did not influence the results of this study. This scoping review was conducted alongside an MSc dissertation in healthcare education, and was written with an overt educationalist perspective drawing on key theories from education literature to underpin findings. Moreover, broader inclusion and exclusion criteria resulting in a larger pool of returned of publications further differentiate this review from existing work on this topic.</p></sec><sec id="sec4.3"><title>Statement on epistemology</title><p id="p0180">In line with scoping review principles, this study drew upon a broad body of knowledge derived from heterogeneous methodologies in order to describe the relevant literature and answer the stated research question. The literature was explored through configurative synthesis of knowledge, including subjective interpretation. This aligned with a subjectivist epistemology, in which it was accepted that there is no universal objective truth, thus meaning is constructed by individuals through interactions with others and the surroundings.<xref rid="bib88" ref-type="bibr"><sup>88</sup></xref> This epistemological position was adopted alongside the interpretivist research paradigm, in which it is acknowledged that learning is influenced by context and environment, highlighting the importance of social interactions in the individual allocation of meaning and creation of knowledge.<xref rid="bib89" ref-type="bibr"><sup>89</sup></xref> These assumptions aligned well with scoping review conventions, which typically eschew objectivist standpoints in favour of the interpretivist paradigm.<xref rid="bib90" ref-type="bibr"><sup>90</sup></xref> In openly adopting these philosophical stances, it is recognised that this review presents a subjective and interpretative synthesis of the knowledge base, and maintains axiological integrity with the stated research aims.<xref rid="bib38" ref-type="bibr"><sup>38</sup></xref><sup>,</sup>
<xref rid="bib91" ref-type="bibr"><sup>91</sup></xref></p></sec><sec id="sec4.4"><title>Limitations and risks</title><p id="p0185">The majority of studies included in this review were small scale and single site, with subsequently limited generalisability. Multiple studies identified a need for larger-scale studies in this field, recommending robust RCT methodology. Of the larger studies and existing reviews included in this scoping review, primary outcome measures were typically clinical in nature, focussing on procedural success and clinical utility rather than specific educational outcomes. Risks associated with innovative technologies included ethical considerations related to storage and use of patient data, validation of innovative technologies in wider patient groups (specifically the extremes of age and those with complex anatomy), and clinician overreliance. Several of the studies which identified a role for innovative technologies in providing supervision to non-experts suggested that AI should not be a complete substitute for expert clinician supervision and emphasised ethical quandaries related to liability in the face of decision-making and clinical error. The concept of novelty and innovation in medical education also needs to be considered. Innovative technologies are defined for this review in <xref rid="tbl1" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref>, but as technologies become more widely adopted, they inevitably cease to be considered as novel. Lastly, limitations as a solo researcher resulted in an inability to triangulate methodological judgements with other authors. In response to this, all exclusion judgements were reflexively recorded and are provided in <xref rid="appsec1" ref-type="sec">Supplementary Appendix 1</xref> to demonstrate consistency and transparency.</p></sec></sec><sec id="sec5"><title>Conclusions</title><p id="p0190">This scoping review identified several educational applications and benefits for using innovative technologies in regional anaesthesia education. These technologies can address existing barriers to learning through creation of further learning opportunities and augmentation of existing educational practices. They also have potential roles in simulation fidelity, clinical supervision and assessment. However heterogeneity, poor reporting, and lack of primary evidence in the literature were identified. Further work including large-scale RCT studies with primary educational outcomes is recommended.</p></sec><sec sec-type="COI-statement" id="coi0010"><title>Declarations of interest</title><p id="p0195">The authors declare that they have no conflicts of interest.</p></sec></body><back><ref-list id="cebib0010"><title>References</title><ref id="bib1"><label>1</label><element-citation publication-type="book" id="sref1"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Mulroy</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name></person-group><part-title>A history of regional anesthesia</part-title><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Eger</surname><given-names>Ii EI.</given-names></name><name><surname>Saidman</surname><given-names>L.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Westhorpe</surname><given-names>R.N.</given-names></name></person-group><source>The Wondrous story Of Anesthesia</source><year>2014</year><publisher-name>Springer</publisher-name><publisher-loc>NY</publisher-loc><fpage>859</fpage><lpage>870</lpage></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib2"><label>2</label><element-citation publication-type="journal" id="sref2"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Turbitt</surname><given-names>L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Mariano</surname><given-names>E.</given-names></name><name><surname>El-Boghdadly</surname><given-names>K.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Future directions in regional anaesthesia: not just for the cognoscenti</article-title><source>Anaesthesia</source><volume>75</volume><year>2020</year><fpage>293</fpage><lpage>297</l